uvc: optionally fill isochronous requests instead of spreading them

Since 6.13, uvc_video_prep_requests() sizes USB requests so that one video
frame occupies exactly one frame interval:

	nreq = DIV_ROUND_UP(video->interval, 2^(bInterval-1) * 1250)
	req_payload_size = (bytesused + nreq * 12) / nreq

At 30 fps on a high-speed gadget that is 267 requests of about 50 bytes, one
per 125 us microframe. Isochronous slots arrive at a fixed 8 kHz whatever we
plan, so 267 requests is also 33.375 ms of wire time for a 33.33 ms frame
period - a 100.1% duty cycle, with no slack in which a missed slot can be made
up.

Every one of those 267 slots has to land, and dwc2 on a Pi Zero W does not
manage it. A missed slot completes -EXDEV; uvc_video_complete() puts the
request straight back on req_free WITHOUT resending it, and queue->buf_used was
already advanced when the request was encoded, so those bytes are gone. f_uvc
raises UVC_QUEUE_DROP_INCOMPLETE, uvc_video_encode_isoc() ends the buffer where
it stands, and uvc_video_encode_header() sets UVC_STREAM_ERR on the rest of the
frame - which the host turns into buf->error, so the frame is discarded there
too.

Measured 2026-08-15, 1280x720 H.264 at 30 fps over dwc2:

	frames in the recording			689
	frames arriving as 39-63 byte stubs	 96   in bursts of six
	packets carrying UVC_STREAM_ERR		213   ~1.9 per stub frame

Before 6.13 - and in 5.10 and 6.12, which this hardware ran without any of this
- requests were simply filled to the endpoint's maxpacket. The same 12 KB frame
is then 12 requests of 1024 bytes: 1.5 ms of wire followed by 31.8 ms idle,
about 22x fewer slots to miss, and the frame is clear of the endpoint long
before the next one arrives.

This restores that as an opt-in, off by default:

	echo 1 > /sys/module/usb_f_uvc/parameters/fixed_req_size

read at STREAMON by uvc_video_alloc_requests(). It is deliberately not the
default: upstream's planner exists to keep a frame's bandwidth demand flat
across the interval, which matters on a shared bus and does not on a cable with
one device on it.

The only code needed is in uvc_video_prep_requests(). Setting reqs_per_frame to
0 already makes uvc_buffer_prepare() use max_req_size for req_payload_size
instead of dividing the frame by a request count - that branch is in the tree
already.

NOT YET MEASURED ON HARDWARE. The numbers above are from the default path.

Signed-off-by: DashBerry <dashberry@localhost>

--- a/drivers/usb/gadget/function/uvc_video.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/gadget/function/uvc_video.c
@@ -9,6 +9,7 @@
 #include <linux/kernel.h>
 #include <linux/device.h>
 #include <linux/errno.h>
+#include <linux/moduleparam.h>
 #include <linux/usb/ch9.h>
 #include <linux/usb/gadget.h>
 #include <linux/usb/video.h>
@@ -494,6 +495,51 @@
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/*
+ * Fill every isochronous request to the endpoint's maxpacket and drain a frame
+ * as fast as the endpoint will take it, instead of spreading it evenly across
+ * the frame interval. This is what f_uvc did before 6.13 (and 5.10, and 6.12).
+ *
+ * The 6.13 planner sizes requests so that a frame occupies exactly one frame
+ * interval: nreq = interval / (2^(bInterval-1) * 1250). At 30 fps on a
+ * high-speed gadget that is 267 requests of ~50 bytes, one per 125 us
+ * microframe, and since slots arrive at a fixed 8 kHz the frame needs 33.375 ms
+ * of wire for a 33.33 ms period - a 100.1% duty cycle. Every one of those 267
+ * slots must land, and dwc2 on a Pi Zero W does not manage that: a missed slot
+ * completes -EXDEV, the request's bytes are NOT resent (uvc_video_complete()
+ * returns it straight to req_free with buf_used already advanced), f_uvc raises
+ * UVC_QUEUE_DROP_INCOMPLETE, and uvc_video_encode_isoc() ends the buffer where
+ * it stands. Measured 2026-08-15 on 720p30 H.264: 96 of 689 frames arrived as
+ * 39-63 byte stubs and 213 packets carried UVC_STREAM_ERR.
+ *
+ * Filling requests instead makes the same 12 KB frame 12 requests of 1024 bytes
+ * - 1.5 ms of wire, then 31.8 ms idle. There are ~22x fewer slots to miss and
+ * the frame is clear of the endpoint long before the next one arrives. That is
+ * why 5.10 was not affected by any of this.
+ *
+ * Off by default: this is a deliberate divergence from upstream, whose planner
+ * exists to keep a frame's bandwidth demand flat across the interval - which
+ * matters on a shared bus and does not on a cable with one device on it.
+ *
+ *      echo 1 > /sys/module/usb_f_uvc/parameters/fixed_req_size
+ *
+ * before the host starts streaming; uvc_video_alloc_requests() reads it at
+ * STREAMON.
+ */
+static bool fixed_req_size;
+module_param(fixed_req_size, bool, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(fixed_req_size,
+	"Fill isochronous requests to maxpacket instead of spreading a frame across the frame interval (pre-6.13 behaviour)");
+
+/*
+ * The pool only has to keep the endpoint fed while the pump refills it, not
+ * hold a whole frame, so cap it rather than taking DIV_ROUND_UP(imagesize,
+ * max_req_size) - imagesize is the DECLARED sizeimage (921600 for 1280x720),
+ * which would allocate 900 requests for frames measured at 24 KB. 64 requests
+ * is 64 KB, 2.6x the largest frame measured, and 8 ms of endpoint time.
+ */
+#define UVCG_FIXED_REQ_POOL 64
+
 static void
 uvc_video_prep_requests(struct uvc_video *video)
 {
@@ -512,6 +558,19 @@
 
 		return;
 	}
+
+	if (fixed_req_size) {
+		video->req_size = max_req_size;
+		video->uvc_num_requests = UVCG_FIXED_REQ_POOL;
+		/*
+		 * reqs_per_frame == 0 is what makes uvc_buffer_prepare() set
+		 * req_payload_size to max_req_size rather than dividing the
+		 * frame by a request count - the branch is already there.
+		 */
+		video->reqs_per_frame = 0;
+
+		return;
+	}
 
 	interval_duration = 1 << (video->ep->desc->bInterval - 1);
 	if (cdev->gadget->speed < USB_SPEED_HIGH)
